   {
    "id": "measles",
    "fr": "Rougeole",
    "en": "Measles",
    "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/measles/index.html",
    "updated": null,
    "text": [
     {
      "text": "Cases of measles are on the rise worldwide. Travelers are at risk of measles if they have not been fully vaccinated at least two weeks prior to departure, or have not had measles in the past, and travel internationally to areas where measles is spreading."
     },
     {
      "text": "All international travelers should be fully vaccinated against measles with the measles-mumps-rubella (MMR) vaccine, including an early dose for infants 6–11 months, according to CDC’s measles vaccination recommendations for international travel."
     }
    ],
    "references": [
     {
      "title": "Measles (Rubeola) - CDC Yellow Book",
      "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/yellow-book/hcp/travel-associated-infections-diseases/measles-rubeola.html"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "mpox",
    "fr": "Mpox (variole du singe)",
    "en": "Monkeypox",
    "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/monkeypox/about/index.html",
    "updated": null,
    "text": [
     {
      "text": "Monkeypox vaccination is recommended for any adult who anticipates any of the following activities during travel:"
     },
     {
      "bullet": "Sexual or intimate contact with a new partner"
     },
     {
      "bullet": "Sexual or intimate contact at a commercial sex venue, like a sex club or bathhouse"
     },
     {
      "bullet": "Sexual or intimate contact in exchange for money, goods, drugs, or other trade"
     },
     {
      "bullet": "Sexual or intimate contact in association with a large public event, such as a rave, party, or festival"
     },
     {
      "text": "Get your first monkeypox vaccine at least 6 weeks before traveling if you can, or as far as possible before travel if fewer than 6 weeks before you leave. Two doses of vaccine, given 4 weeks apart, are recommended. After completing your first and second vaccine doses it takes about 2 more weeks to get the best protection against monkeypox. Whether or not you've been vaccinated, continue to take steps to reduce your risk of getting monkeypox while you are away."
     }
    ],
    "references": [
     {
      "title": "Monkeypox Vaccination | Monkeypox | CDC",
      "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/monkeypox/vaccines/index.html"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "rabies",
    "fr": "Rage",
    "en": "Rabies",
    "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/rabies/about/index.html",
    "updated": null,
    "text": [
     {
      "text": "Dogs infected with rabies are not commonly found in Greenland."
     },
     {
      "text": "Rabies is present in some terrestrial wildlife species."
     },
     {
      "text": "If rabies exposures occur while in Greenland, rabies vaccines may only be available in larger suburban/urban medical facilities."
     },
     {
      "text": "Rabies pre-exposure vaccination considerations include whether travelers 1) will be performing occupational or recreational activities that increase risk for exposure to potentially rabid animals and 2) might have difficulty getting prompt access to safe post-exposure prophylaxis."
     },
     {
      "text": "Please consult with a healthcare provider to determine whether you should receive pre-exposure vaccination before travel."
     },
     {
      "text": "For more information, see country rabies status assessments."
     }
    ],
    "references": [
